<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kim Tu-bong</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1889">1889</a>–<a href="/years/1958">1958</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Kim Doo-bong</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kim Tu-bong was a Korean linguist and communist politician who led the Yanan faction of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koreans">Koreans</a> returning from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> in 1945. He was vice chairman of the Provisional People's Committee for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-korea">North Korea</a>, the first chairman of the Workers' Party of North Korea and later head of the standing committee of the Supreme People's Assembly. He was purged by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kim-il-sung">Kim Il Sung</a> in 1958.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kim-tu-bong.jpg" alt="Kim Tu-bong" width="315" height="437" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Government of North Korea · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kim_Tu-bong_2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Kim Tu-bong was born in 1889 in South Gyeongsang Province near Busan and was homeschooled in his early years. He moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seoul">Seoul</a> around 1908 and attended Bosung and other schools. There he worked closely with the linguist Chu Sigyŏng, who was beginning his study of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hangul">Hangul</a>, the script made by King Sejong in the 15th century. Kim also worked as a teacher and joined the Korea Youth organisation in 1913. In 1916 he spent much of his time compiling the Malmoi, the first Korean dictionary. He is remembered in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-korea">South Korea</a> above all for his work in establishing the Korean linguistic field, especially on Hangul.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/march-first-movement">March First Movement</a> of 1919 he and other members of an independence club fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and in April 1919 set up a provisional government in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanghai">Shanghai</a>. There he met <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communism">communism</a> and accepted it in 1920, after first supporting the Democratic Party. In 1924 he became president of a children's education <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a>. After the Japanese invasion of China he went with other members of the Korean government to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yanan">Yan'an</a>, headquarters of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chinese-communist-party">Chinese Communist Party</a>, where he headed the independence club.</p><p>He returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> in December 1945 and settled in the north under Soviet occupation. In February 1946 he became chairman of the New People's Party, formed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yanan">Yan'an</a> faction, which merged that year into the Workers' Party of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-korea">North Korea</a>, and he became its first chairman. He was first head of state of North Korea, as chairman of the standing committee of the Supreme People's Assembly, from 1948 to 1957, though <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kim-il-sung">Kim Il Sung</a> held real power.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korean-war">Korean War</a> he was purged, accused of involvement in the August Faction Incident of 1956, and disappeared without record of his fate. He is believed to have been executed or to have died in detention, perhaps in the 1960s.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kim Tu-bong” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In February 1946 the Soviet Civil Administration convened a meeting of political parties and local people's committees in Pyongyang, which created the Provisional People's Committee for North Korea, with Kim Il Sung as chairman and <b>Kim Tu-bong</b> as vice chairman.</blockquote><a href="/state/provisional-peoples-committee-for-north-korea">Provisional People's Committee for North Korea</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Busan, in what is now <a href="/countries/south-korea">South Korea</a>. Died in South Pyongan Province, in what is now <a href="/countries/north-korea">North Korea</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=35.18,129.08&amp;year=1933" aria-label="Busan on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Kim Tu-bong"><image href="/maps/globes/g120n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5632 4533h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5632 4533h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=35.18,129.08&amp;year=1933">What was here in 1933? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
